 {"status": "complete", "id": "AUG-147", "title": "<include src> is decorative for selection: every *.xml joins the bundle whether included or not, so removing an include changes nothing", "detail": {"fingerprint": "d2e7073ebbfa47aa2bc40c34d39919ced6cd6c1694521b03ca78a2cd069254c7", "evidence": [{"run_id": "c03a74b9", "ts": "2026-11-13T10:19:26.850135+00:00"}]}, "priority": "P2", "source": null, "reasoning": "Deep pass 2026-11-05 at cf5bfe2, three measured arms: (a) a corpus where user.xml has <include src=\"shared.xml\"/> selects INC-SH-1 and INC-U-1; (b) delete ONLY the include declaration -> the identical selection, INC-SH-1 still present; (c) a file nothing includes (orphan.xml) still contributes FLAT-R2 to the bundle. The loader rglobs every *.xml under the root, so the corpus is one flat pool and <include> only adds a failure mode (a dangling include is refused). The PRD's R4 and §1 present include as the reuse mechanism and the shipped fixtures describe it as splicing; neither the selection nor the closure depends on it.", "review_notes": null, "ts": null, "acceptance_criteria": "PASS: the documented semantics match the code: either <include> governs which documents participate (an un-included file's blocks are NOT selectable) or the PRD/README say plainly that the root is a flat pool and <include> is an assertion-only edge.\nPASS: if includes are assertion-only, a test asserts the intended consequence in both directions — removing an include from a corpus changes nothing, and a block whose document is included is reachable without listing it explicitly.\nPASS: duplicate detection still fires when an included document and an includer declare the same id (no silent double-splice), proven by a fixture.\nPASS: the choice is recorded in docs (one paragraph naming the rule) so authors stop believing removal of an include is a breaking change.", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-05T18:24:13.515088+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-11-13T10:19:26.850135+00:00", "fore